This is a strong workflow foundation. The main opportunity is to turn it from a reliable demo into an efficient clinical product.
Priority	Improvement	Why it matters
P0	Add server-side appointment lists: “my appointments,” doctor daily/upcoming schedule, and admin operational queue—filtered, paginated, searchable	The current doctor/admin dashboards explicitly rely on browser-local history and ID lookup, which is not usable in real operations.
P0	Build a clinician workspace: today’s schedule, patient context, one-click complete/no-show, schedule/leave management	This removes staff dependence on appointment IDs and makes the system practical at reception and in clinics.
P0	Replace doctor-ID entry with doctor search, specialty/location filters, and a calendar-style slot picker	Booking by opaque doctor ID is the biggest patient usability issue.
P1	Add self-service reschedule, cancellation reasons, and a waitlist that fills newly released slots	Reduces reception work, no-shows, and unused clinical capacity. Keep rescheduling as a new durable workflow or carefully compensate the old reservation.
P1	Send actionable reminders: secure one-tap confirm/cancel links, patient notification preferences, then optional SMS/WhatsApp fallback	The backend has good email reliability, but requiring a login to confirm adds friction and increases no-response releases.
P1	Create a real admin operations dashboard: global counts, failed reminders, stuck workflows, reconciliation findings, schedule conflicts	The system already produces much of this signal, but it is currently available mainly through logs, Prometheus, and Temporal UI.
P1	Add stronger identity controls before public launch: password reset, MFA/SSO for staff, refresh/revocation strategy, session/device management	The project itself documents that refresh tokens, revocation, asymmetric keys, and key rotation are not yet implemented.
P2	Make the data model multi-clinic-ready: clinicId, clinic timezone, locations, specialties, rooms, and provider capacity	The current design is deliberately single-clinic and one-timezone. Add tenancy before expanding, rather than retrofitting it after patient data grows.
P2	Add patient-facing accessibility and language support: simple copy, locale-aware dates, screen-reader checks, translated reminder templates	Healthcare booking is used by a broad range of patients; this improves completion more than visual polish alone.
P2	Add outcome measurement: booking conversion, slot utilization, cancellation/no-show rate, reminder delivery/confirmation rate, median time to resolve failures	These are the metrics needed to decide which UX and notification changes actually save money.


For cost optimisation, I’d take this approach:
- Keep Temporal for the long-running confirmation/reminder lifecycle; it is solving a real durability problem well.
- For a small clinic, use managed PostgreSQL, Redis, and Temporal rather than operating a production Temporal cluster yourself. The staff time and incident risk usually outweigh infrastructure savings.
- Run API and worker as independently autoscaled services. Scale workers on Temporal task-queue backlog and schedule-to-start latency, not CPU alone.
- Add database indexes based on the new list screens—especially (doctorId, appointmentTime) and filtered/indexed queues for active appointment and reminder work.
- Use email as the default low-cost channel; reserve SMS/WhatsApp for opt-in, high-value reminders or patients who do not confirm email.
- Replace repeated workflow-status polling with server-sent events or a small notification channel once traffic grows. The current bounded polling is sensible for a demo, but creates unnecessary API/Temporal reads at scale.
- Set retention policies for audit logs, observability data, workflow visibility/history, and expired appointments according to your clinical/legal requirements.
Recommended delivery order:
1. Appointment list APIs plus doctor/admin dashboards.
2. Doctor search and calendar booking UI.
3. Reschedule, waitlist, and actionable confirmation links.
4. Patient preferences and multichannel reminders.
5. Multi-clinic/room/specialty model, analytics, and advanced identity controls.
I made no code changes. Backend type-checking and frontend type-checking/linting pass. The local Temporal workflow test suite reported its 13 lifecycle tests as skipped, so I would also make a containerized Temporal test environment mandatory in developer and CI verification.



## Security action so far and what next


| Security area | Current protection |
|---|---|
| Passwords | Stored as bcrypt hashes, not plain text |
| Login | JWT token is issued only after password verification |
| Protected APIs | Backend requires `Authorization: Bearer <token>` |
| Roles | Patient, doctor, and admin permissions are checked in backend code |
| Data access | A doctor cannot request another doctor’s appointments; patients cannot request another patient’s data |
| Input validation | Zod validates request bodies, IDs, dates, filters, and page limits |
| Abuse protection | Login, booking, confirmation, cancellation, and management APIs are rate-limited with Redis |
| HTTP headers | Helmet adds common browser security headers |
| Logs | Passwords, tokens, API keys, and database URLs are redacted |
| Audit trail | Appointment changes create audit-log records |
| Production config | Production rejects unsafe DB/Redis/Temporal connection settings and weak JWT secrets |
| Database safety | Prisma parameterized queries reduce SQL-injection risk |


1. JWT tokens are stored in browser sessionStorage.
   This is better than persistent localStorage, but JavaScript can still read it if an XSS vulnerability occurs. Move authentication to HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookies or use a Backend-for-Frontend pattern. OWASP specifically recommends avoiding JWT/session-token storage in browser web-storage APIs. OWASP Session Management guidance
2. No MFA for doctors and admins.
   Staff accounts should use MFA, especially because they can access patient appointment information and update outcomes. OWASP Authentication guidance
3. No password reset or account recovery flow.
   A safe reset process needs one-time, expiring tokens, generic responses, rate limits, and audit logging. OWASP Forgot Password guidance
4. JWT revocation and rotation are missing.
   A stolen token remains valid until its expiry. Add refresh-token rotation, logout revocation, key rotation, and forced logout after sensitive account changes.
5. Local Docker setup is not encrypted.
   This is acceptable only for development. Production must use HTTPS for users, TLS to PostgreSQL/Redis/Temporal, encrypted backups, secret management, and restricted network access.
6. Healthcare compliance controls are incomplete.
   You would still need formal access reviews, data-retention rules, backup recovery tests, security monitoring, penetration testing, vendor agreements, and compliance requirements applicable to your country/organisation.
